  "page": "vision/index.html",
  "strings": {
    "Vision | From one agent to an AI-native organization | PassionCode.ai": "",
    "Why agents rot when nothing holds them together, what a harness is, and how PassionCode.ai grows one agent into a family, a team and an organization. Local first, open source, on top of Claude Code, Codex and what comes next.": "",
    "The PassionCode.ai vision": "",
    "Agents need a harness. How one agent grows into a family, a team and an organization, and what works today.": "",
    "From one agent to an AI-native organization": "",
    "Why agents need a harness, how one agent grows into a family, a team and an organization, and what PassionCode.ai offers today.": "",
    "Sergey": "",
    "Does PassionCode.ai replace Claude Code or Codex?": "",
    "No. It works on top of them. Your coding agent keeps doing the work; PassionCode.ai gives it a home, accounts, health, memory and evidence, and lets the next agent join the same family.": "",
    "Is any of this a promise?": "",
    "No. Each stage on this page is marked available now or direction. The tools listed as available can be downloaded today; the rest is where we are building, in that order.": "",
    "Does PassionCode.ai score employees?": "",
    "No. Analytics are about agents and the outcomes of processes: what ran, what it cost, what was approved. Nothing watches people in secret, and what is measured is visible to the people it concerns.": "",
    "The vision · as of October 2026": "",
    "From one agent to an": "",
    "AI-native organization": "",
    "Agents are easy to start and hard to keep. We are building the harness that holds them together, so each new agent makes the whole family stronger instead of adding one more thing to look after": "",
    "Local first, open source, vendor-neutral": "",
    "On top of Claude Code, Codex and what comes next": "",
    "On this page": "",
    "Agent sprawl": "",
    "The missing harness": "",
    "What we believe": "",
    "The path": "",
    "Today and direction": "",
    "Trust": "",
    "01 / AGENT SPRAWL": "",
    "Agents multiply faster": "",
    "than anyone can hold them": "",
    "Every team can start an agent in an afternoon. Few can say a month later which agents run, on which accounts, what they cost, what they changed and whether anyone still needs them. Agents built alone do not talk to each other, and the ones nobody looks after quietly rot.": "",
    "MCKINSEY · NOVEMBER 2025": "",
    "Many experiment, few scale": "",
    "62% of organizations surveyed are at least experimenting with AI agents, yet in any single business function no more than 10% say they are scaling them.": "",
    "GARTNER · JUNE 2025": "",
    "Many projects will stop": "",
    "Gartner predicts that over 40% of agentic AI projects will be canceled by the end of 2027, because of rising costs, unclear business value or inadequate risk controls.": "",
    "GARTNER · APRIL 2026": "",
    "Sprawl is the next problem": "",
    "By 2028 an average global Fortune 500 enterprise will have over 150,000 agents in use, Gartner predicts, while only 13% of organizations think they have the right agent governance in place.": "",
    "FORTUNE · AUGUST 2025": "",
    "The gap is learning, not models": "",
    "A report from the NANDA initiative at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology found most generative AI pilots stalling with no measurable effect on profit and loss, and named the cause a learning gap: tools that do not adapt to the way a company works.": "",
    "Sources": "",
    "McKinsey, The state of AI in 2025: Agents, innovation, and transformation": "",
    "· November 2025": "",
    "Gartner press release, 25 June 2025": "",
    "Gartner press release, 28 April 2026": "",
    "Fortune, 18 August 2025": "",
    ", on the report The GenAI Divide: State of AI in Business 2025. A caveat: the report rests on 150 interviews, a survey of 350 employees and a review of 300 public deployments, and its headline share of failing pilots has been questioned for how it defines success. Read it as a signal, not a measurement.": "",
    "02 / THE MISSING HARNESS": "",
    "The model is not the problem": "",
    "What holds it is": "",
    "A harness": "",
    "is everything around a model that makes an agent dependable over time: where it works, which tools and accounts it may use, what it remembers between sessions, the checks its work must pass, and the record of what it did. The labs that build the models use the same word: Anthropic writes about": "",
    "effective harnesses for long-running agents": "",
    "(November 2025), and OpenAI about": "",
    "harness engineering": "",
    "(February 2026).": "",
    "A home": "",
    "Each project has one place for its purpose, board, decisions and releases, shared by every agent that works on it. That is Fabric.": "",
    "Accounts": "",
    "Each agent runs on the account it should, with its limits in view. That is Fabric Switchboard.": "",
    "Health and control": "",
    "Every agent service, its state, spend and updates, in one window that people and agents can drive. That is Fabric Dashboards.": "",
    "Memory and evidence": "",
    "What changed, what was decided and what needs attention, with the proof beside it. That is Project Observatory.": "",
    "One contract ties them together: the open": "",
    ". The Fabric Agent Adapter skill teaches any coding agent to build to it, so an agent you make today joins the family without a rewrite.": "",
    "03 / WHAT WE BELIEVE": "",
    "Six principles": "",
    "we build by": "",
    "Agents you own": "",
    "They run on your machines and your accounts, in code anyone can read under the GNU AGPL-3.0.": "",
    "Any agent": "",
    "Vendor-neutral. A harness on top of Claude Code, Codex and whatever comes next, never a replacement for them.": "",
    "Grow by need": "",
    "Start with one agent for one job. Add the next one when the work asks for it, not because a plan said so.": "",
    "Evidence, not trust": "",
    "Every action leaves a record you can read, and done means checked, not reported.": "",
    "People decide": "",
    "Agents prepare, check and carry the work. People set what they may do and approve what matters.": "",
    "Analytics are about agents and the outcomes of processes. Nothing watches people in secret.": "",
    "04 / THE PATH": "",
    "Six stages": "",
    "Each one useful on its own": "",
    "The same family grows from one person to a whole organization. Each stage says whether it works today or is where we are building.": "",
    "Your coding agent builds an agent for one job, with a contract, a dashboard and tests.": "",
    "Solo founder": "",
    "an agent that drafts replies to app store reviews every morning": "",
    "Small team": "",
    "an agent that writes release notes from merged pull requests": "",
    "Department": "",
    "an agent that assembles the weekly report from your own data": "",
    "Agents connected to Fabric share one project’s board, memory and hand-offs, so one picks up where the other stopped.": "",
    "the review agent hands a bug report to the coding agent": "",
    "one agent writes the change, another reviews it": "",
    "a support agent passes a refund question to finance, with the evidence": "",
    "build, store listing, screenshots and submission, one approval": "",
    "brief, creative variants, brand check, launch": "",
    "invoices gathered, reconciled and prepared for sign-off": "",
    "Dashboards shows each agent’s health and spend, the tools update themselves after checking their signatures, and Observatory keeps what changed. Agents watching over each other is the direction.": "",
    "one window says which agent stopped and why": "",
    "a spend limit stops an agent before the bill does": "",
    "every change traced to the agent and the person who approved it": "",
    "People join the same loops: each gets a workplace, the agents of their role and what they may do.": "",
    "a first hire inherits the agents, not a pile of scripts": "",
    "designers, engineers and marketers share one family": "",
    "a workplace per role, rolled out after a measured pilot": "",
    "Agents reach every workplace. People act as the experts who verify agents’ work and build agents of their own, and those agents spread through the network.": "",
    "the company grows without growing the routine": "",
    "a good agent built by one person reaches everyone": "",
    "leaders see how processes run, not how hard people look busy": "",
    "05 / TODAY AND DIRECTION": "",
    "What you can use today": "",
    "and what we are building": "",
    "Download and use": "",
    "The PassionCode.ai launcher and the Fabric Agent Adapter skill, to build or convert agents with Claude Code or Codex": "",
    "Fabric Dashboards, Fabric Switchboard and Project Observatory, released": "",
    "Fabric, an early preview: projects, boards, decisions and connected agents; its conversation does not reply yet": "",
    "Fabric Inbox, a development preview for mail": "",
    "Being built, in this order": "",
    "Fabric creating agents from a conversation": "",
    "Chains of agents with a person’s approval inside them": "",
    "Agents that watch over each other’s health": "",
    "Workplaces for teams, and PassionCode for Enterprise for organizations": "",
    "More coding agents connected to Fabric, listed on": "",
    "the agents page": "",
    "06 / TRUST": "",
    "What you can rely on": "",
    "at every stage": "",
    "Security": "",
    "Each agent gets only the tools and accounts you allow. Keys stay in protected local storage; releases are signed and checked before they install.": "",
    "Data": "",
    "Your work stays on your computers and your cloud accounts. Fabric and Switchboard send only anonymous usage counts, and you can turn them off.": "",
    "No lock-in": "",
    "Open source under the GNU AGPL-3.0, an open contract, any coding agent and your own model accounts. Leave whenever you like and keep everything.": "",
    "Reliability": "",
    "Every tool has a public test command and release notes, and says plainly what does not work yet. Done means checked against evidence.": "",
    "People": "",
    "Analytics are about agents and outcomes: what ran, what it cost, what was approved. Nothing is stealth, and what is measured is visible to the people it concerns.": "",
    "A harness needs discipline inside each change, too. We recommend": "",
    "family: it carries a change through gated stages, from the brief and the plan to the tests, the deploy and the acceptance, and does not move on until each gate passes. It works in Claude Code, Codex and other agents that read Agent Skills.": "",
    "07 / FOR ORGANIZATIONS": "",
    "The same path,": "",
    "for a whole organization": "",
    "We bring organizations into AI the way the path above runs: a measured pilot first, then a workplace per role, then agents on employees’ machines, with people as the experts who verify the work and build agents of their own. PassionCode for Enterprise adds analytics on agents and processes, control and policy, help for employees to automate their work, and a choice of your cloud or ours.": "",
    "What organizations get": "",
    "A pilot measured against your baseline": "",
    "one process, hours and cost, every action recorded": "",
    "Workplaces rolled out per role": "",
    "the agents, the accounts and what they may do": "",
    "what ran, what it cost, what was approved": "",
    "and the models on your own provider accounts": "",
    "QUESTIONS": "",
    "Before you start": "",
    "START WITH ONE AGENT": "",
    "The family starts": "",
    "with one command": "",
    "Install the skills, build the first agent, and let the rest join when the work asks for it.": ""
  }
}
